course: dictionary
lesson: 10930
title: What is a voucher?
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A voucher is a piece of paper or a code that you can swap for something. It might give you a thing for free, a service, or money off a price. Think of it as a ticket that says, "This counts for something."
explain: «In the UK children under 16 get free NHS eye tests and vouchers towards glasses.» The vouchers help to pay for glasses, so they save money. The sentence also tells you who can get them: children under 16.
explain: A discount voucher takes some money off a price. A gift voucher is given as a present, and you spend it in a shop. Read the words on a voucher to see what it can really do for you.
fact: A voucher is a piece of paper or code you can exchange for goods or services.
fact: A discount voucher gives you money off a price.
fact: A gift voucher is often given as a present, for example at a birthday.
fact: «Vouchers towards glasses» means the voucher helps pay for glasses.
fact: Phrases to know: gift voucher, discount voucher.
exercise: Aisha gets a voucher that takes £5 off her shopping. What kind of voucher is this? | *A discount voucher | A cinema ticket | A bus timetable | A library card
exercise: «In the UK children under 16 get vouchers towards glasses.» What do the vouchers help to pay for? | *Paying for glasses | Buying sweets | Painting a picture | Travelling to school
exercise: Which words best describe a voucher? | A large box for toys | A type of school uniform | *A piece of paper or code you can swap for goods or services | A coin from another country
test: Ben is given a gift voucher for his birthday. What is the voucher for? | *Spending in a shop | Throwing away after one day | Sending to a friend by post | Keeping as a school book
test: Which sentence uses the word voucher correctly? | I voucher my dinner every morning | *I used my discount voucher to pay less for my new trainers | The bus driver checked my voucher and let me on for free | The voucher is a loud animal that lives in the sea
exam: Mia has a voucher worth £10 and wants a £14 jacket. Which is the best way to explain what she needs to do? | Ask for a £14 voucher instead | *Use the voucher and pay the other £4 with money or a card | Use the voucher, then pay the other £4 | Throw the voucher away and pay £14
